/// Temporary compatibility marker for resource-owning types.
///
/// New code declares `func =(self: &var T, other: &T);` to prohibit copying and implements
/// `func ~T(self: &var T)` for cleanup. `Drop` remains during the staged package migration so existing implementations
/// stay move-only and continue to be destroyed. It will be removed after every first-party owner uses the canonical
/// operations.
///
/// The compiler runs cleanup once per value that was not moved away, before fields, elements, and enum payloads are
/// released in reverse construction order. That includes ordinary scope exit, early returns, loop exits, propagation,
/// and the value an assignment replaces.
///
/// Panic and process termination do not unwind, so `Drop` does not run on those paths. A resource whose release can
/// fail should therefore also offer an explicit `Close` that consumes the value and reports the error; `Drop` has no
/// way to report one.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop
interface Drop {
    /// Releases whatever this value owns.
    ///
    /// Legacy implementations borrow the value mutably; canonical code should use `~Type` instead.
    ///
    /// Must not fail and must not panic, because there is no caller to report to and no way to stop the destruction
    /// already under way.
    ///
    /// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop/drop
    func Drop();
}
